    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d355-a1">

                <pb facs="0366" n="360" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg360"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.—Personal.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-12-20"><hi rendition="#small-caps">December</hi> 20,
                            1894</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Lord Kimberley</hi>: But for the announcement in
                    the newspapers of your absence from town for the holidays I should come this
                    morning to show you a telegram I received just now from Secretary Gresham,
                    stating that the commander of U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Columbia</hi> had
                    telegraphed from Jamaica that he had there been informed that “Clarence,” the
                    titular head of the Mosquito tribe, had been notified to hold himself in
                    readiness to be returned to Bluefields, and that recognition of Nicaraguan
                    order, and the late action of the inhabitants of the Mosquito region
                    incorporating themselves with the rest of Nicaragua, was refused by the British
                    authorities or those representing them.</p>
                <p>I need hardly say that Mr. Gresham naturally discredits such reports; but I would
                    be glad to have a word from you to set them at rest authoritatively.</p>
                <p>Believe me, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
